🐛 Bug
Hi.
I am trying to implement a custom likelihood function for use in SVGP. The aim is to train the following model: y=g(f(x))+noise. Here f(x) is the output of the GP and is a latent variable. g(.) is a known function.
To begin, I have tried to implement a basic Gaussian likelihood function. The GPytorch inbuilt function appears to use the function distribution of f(x) directly rather than obtaining samples from it. To extend this to the model above, I need this to work by obtaining samples from f(x) so I can then pass them through g(.). However, the simple version shown below seems to have PSD issues. Can you spot any issues with my code? Thank you.
class LikelihoodTest(Likelihood):
    def __init__(self):

        super(LikelihoodTest,self).__init__()
        self.noise_covar=HomoskedasticNoise()

    def _shaped_noise_covar(self, base_shape):
        return self.noise_covar(shape=base_shape)
    def forward(self, function_samples):
        noise = self._shaped_noise_covar(function_samples.shape).diag()
        return base_distributions.Normal(function_samples, noise.sqrt())
** Stack trace/error message **
NotPSDError: Matrix not positive definite after repeatedly adding jitter up to 1.0e-04.

🚀 Feature Request
I am trying to write a custom Kernel that is very similar to RBF but with the difference of subtracting the pair distances between pair of points instead of subtracting the points themselves.
To be precise, the RBF kernel(https://docs.gpytorch.ai/en/v1.6.0/kernels.html#rbfkernel) computes a covariance matrix based on the squared exponential kernel between inputs x1 and x2.
However, I would like to change the inputs from x1 and x2 to 1/r(x1) and 1/r(x2). Where r(x) is the matrix of distances between all pair points of x1 and r(x2) is the matrix of distances between all pair points of x2. I call it Inverse Euclidian Distance Kernel.
Please see this link for a clear picture of the Kernel: https://drive.google.com/file/d/11tXnyQN-mujCm1LEq9gf2L-wmbAk9w-0/view?usp=sharing
I have defined these two classes below for this purpose:
class InverseEuclidianDistanceKernel(gpytorch.kernels.Kernel):
def init(self, num_dimensions, lengthscale_prior=None, active_dims=None):
super(InverseEuclidianDistanceKernel, self).init(active_dims=active_dims)
self.register_parameter(name="raw_lengthscale", parameter=torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(num_dimensions)))
@property
def lengthscale(self):
    return torch.exp(self.raw_lengthscale)

def forward(self, x1, x2, diag=False, last_dim_is_batch=False):
    if diag:
        return torch.ones(x1.size(-2))

    x1_ = x1.div(self.lengthscale)
    x2_ = x2.div(self.lengthscale)
    x1_ = x1_.unsqueeze(-2)
    x2_ = x2_.unsqueeze(-3)
    # dist = (x1_ - x2_).pow(2).sum(-1) # this would be RBF kernel but I comment it here
    dist_x1, dist_x2 = torch.zeros(x1.size(0), x1.size(0)), torch.zeros(x2.size(0), x2.size(0))
    dist = torch.ones(x1.size(0), x2.size(0))
    dist = (1 / dist1.sum(-1) - 1 / dist2.sum(-1).reshape(-1,1)).pow(2)
    dist = dist.transpose(-1, -2)
    return torch.exp(- dist / 2)

class ExactGPModelwithInverseEuclidianDistanceKernel(gpytorch.models.ExactGP):
def init(self, train_x, train_y, likelihood):
super(ExactGPModelwithInverseEuclidianDistanceKernel, self).init(train_x, train_y, likelihood)
self.mean_module = gpytorch.means.ConstantMean()
self.covar_module = gpytorch.kernels.ScaleKernel(InverseEuclidianDistanceKernel(num_dimensions=2))
def forward(self, x):
    mean_x = self.mean_module(x)
    covar_x = self.covar_module(x)
    return gpytorch.distributions.MultivariateNormal(mean_x, covar_x)

I seem to have completed every part of it however, I am struggling with one aspect of it. The kernel requires a high dimension lengthscale variable that has the size of the distance matrices. For example, if there are 100 input data that are 2d, then lengthscale dimension would 100x100.
I have looked through the documentation of gpytorch and I have not found the part that addresses this issue. As far as I understand, the lengthscale dimension goes as 2 in my case.
Let me know if I am missing anything from gpytorch that covers this.

Motivation
The motivation behind this new kernel is from a reference that is newly published, and it has the promise of outperforming the regular RBF kernel when the system is closer to singularities. This type of kernel could be very important to those studying potential energy surfaces.
I leave a link to the study here: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jctc.9b01038

